WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.427
Before creating embeddings, print a few chunks and read them.

00:00:04.427 --> 00:00:10.102
This step may feel simple, but it catches a surprising number of problems.

00:00:10.102 --> 00:00:15.527
Ask whether the chunk is understandable without seeing the entire original document.

00:00:15.527 --> 00:00:21.262
Check whether important headings or conditions were separated from the sentence they explain.

00:00:21.262 --> 00:00:29.212
Look for duplicated navigation text, broken words, or chunks that contain almost no useful information.

00:00:29.682 --> 00:00:34.884
If a chunk is poor, the embedding model cannot repair the missing context.

00:00:34.884 --> 00:00:43.239
In real projects, I also like to inspect chunks from different document types rather than only the first few rows.

00:00:43.239 --> 00:00:49.364
A PDF, web page, and policy document may behave very differently.

00:00:49.364 --> 00:00:53.264
Think of this as quality control before indexing.

00:00:53.264 --> 00:01:00.429
Once thousands or millions of chunks are embedded, changing the chunking strategy becomes more expensive.

00:01:00.429 --> 00:01:04.164
Good retrieval begins with useful retrieval units.
